If two Scott continuous maps agree on every finite set, then they agree on every element of Pω. This is the uniqueness half of Scott's Theorem 3.5: a continuous realizer of an extensional map is unique, because continuous maps are determined by their values on finite sets.

Rewrite both sides by Scott continuity as Scott unions. An element lies in a Scott piece of one side exactly when it lies in the corresponding piece of the other, because the two maps agree on each finite set.
